WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.840
In 2010, the seventh Saw movie was released&nbsp;
and was dubbed as “The Final Chapter”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.440
and that same year, the creators of the&nbsp;
original Saw, Leigh Whannell and James Wan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.600
created a new franchise called Insidious. On&nbsp;
the chalkboard in the background of Insidious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.400
is a sketch of a familiar face: Billy the Puppet&nbsp;
from the Saw movies accompanied by the number 8.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.960
This was the first ever clue&nbsp;
about the eighth Saw movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.320
which would be released seven&nbsp;
years later and be titled: Jigsaw.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.856
Jigsaw also contains many Easter Eggs of it’s own,&nbsp;
so stick around if you want to hear about them.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.120
Welcome to Things You Missed. I know this is&nbsp;
my third attempt at Things You Missed episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.560
on Jigsaw, but the first two videos were&nbsp;
made while the movie was still in theaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.080
so having the ability to pause and write&nbsp;
down all my notes has given me the time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.080
notice many more Things You Missed, from the&nbsp;
secrets hidden on computer screens to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.880
of the oldest franchise staples being reimagined for&nbsp;
a new decade, lets go ahead and get into them.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.960
It opens with the Lionsgate logo, but unlike&nbsp;
many Saw films, this one uses the regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.360
Lionsgate animation as opposed to the spooky&nbsp;
one. What’s going on here? Did they just forget?

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.960
The first actual shot of the movie is&nbsp;
this rolled up mat of tire spikes used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.200
to slow down a fleeing car, but the starburst&nbsp;
shaped image of the spikes also foreshadows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.440
the final scene of the movie, where we get&nbsp;
a nice blooming bouquet of Halloranhead.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.280
The man trying to escape the police is Edgar&nbsp;
Munsen who has been pressured into finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.080
the trigger that starts the 2016 edition of the&nbsp;
barn game. As he runs, we hear a cinematic cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.786
of the main theme of the franchise: “Hello Zep”,&nbsp;
with the melody being converted into percussion.

00:01:30.786 --> 00:01:36.648
🎵 Chase Edgar - Charlie Clouser 🎵

00:01:36.648 --> 00:01:42.560
“Dun du-nuh. Duh, nuh-nuh… nuh-nuh-nuh!&nbsp;
Dun du-nuh. Duh, nuh-nuh… nuh-nuh-nuh!”

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.920
He gets to the rooftop and finds the&nbsp;
trigger hidden behind a red X. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.560
is a callback to the idea of X marks the spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.808
which was a clue given by Jigsaw to the&nbsp;
players of his games in Saw and Saw 2.

00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:55.005
"X marks the spot."

00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:56.389
"What?"

00:01:56.922 --> 00:02:01.516
 “X marks… marks the spot… for the answer.”

00:02:01.516 --> 00:02:06.400
But it would be Saw 2’s X that most closely&nbsp;
relates to the X seen in Jigsaw. In Saw 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.400
I discussed how the painting that contains the&nbsp;
X was actually a clue about the movie’s twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.520
how the game at the Nerve Gas House&nbsp;
and the police raid of Wilson Steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.120
Plant could not have taken place at the&nbsp;
same time, because this painting exists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.720
in both locations. We eventually find out&nbsp;
that the Nerve Gas House stuff seen on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.640
screens was just playback of events that&nbsp;
had already happened many hours before.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.960
Jigsaw uses the same twist, only the game at&nbsp;
the barn takes place many years before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.880
stuff in the present. It’s unlikely Jigsaw&nbsp;
would use two X markers in the same game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.680
so when we see a second X on&nbsp;
a tractor later in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.040
that’s a clue that this game isn’t the&nbsp;
same game as the one in the present.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.080
That may be the most cryptic clue,&nbsp;
but it certainly isn’t the only one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.040
The movie is actually brimming with&nbsp;
evidence that the barn game we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.480
is John Kramer’s earliest test game&nbsp;
taking place over a decade before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.920
Halloran begins to investigate the&nbsp;
victim referred to as “buckethead”.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.680
When Jigsaw’s voice comes on for the first&nbsp;
time, we don’t see their facial reactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.200
but we can tell that they're scared, as anyone&nbsp;
would be if they were kidnapped. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.200
they never refer to Jigsaw being their&nbsp;
possible captor, because Jigsaw isn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.280
a known figure at this point. This is before&nbsp;
The Bathroom game or the Razor Wire Trap.

00:03:07.814 --> 00:03:14.000
ANNA: “That voice. It said salvation can be&nbsp;
ours if we cleanse our soul of our lies.”

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.400
They aren’t even familiar&nbsp;
with the concept of games yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.000
because the only trap so far was&nbsp;
Cecil, which was never made public.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.500
CARLY: “He said it was a game.”

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:23.393
ANNA: “Well then I hope for&nbsp;
all of our sakes that it is a game.” 

00:03:23.393 --> 00:03:25.090
RYAN: “Oh yeah, why is that sweetheart?”

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.800
 ANNA: “Cause games can be won.”

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:30.800
Everyone acts scared when they see Billy, not&nbsp;
because they recognize him from Jigsaw’s tapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.480
but just because he’s creepy on his own.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.920
"No, that's not creepy at all."

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.240
When Logan and Eleanor examine the first victim,&nbsp;
there is another clue about the massive time gap.

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.760
LOGAN: “The partial decapitation&nbsp;
was made by a circular saw.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.299
Traces of ferric oxide hematite in&nbsp;
the wound.”

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:48.480
ELEANOR: “Rust, from blades.”

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.080
When we saw the blades in the first room,&nbsp;
they looked brand new, but after sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.600
there for over a decade, it makes sense&nbsp;
that they might have accumulated some rust.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.520
There seems to be a moment in the final&nbsp;
room where Anna recognizes John Kramer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.440
but she recognizes him as her next door&nbsp;
neighbor, not as the Jigsaw killer.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.406
“Hello Anna.”

00:04:04.406 --> 00:04:06.881
“John? John Kramer? ”

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:11.520
Then there’s the issue of the victims themselves.&nbsp;
The first victim, “Buckethead” as they call him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.280
was Malcolm Neale, who was a criminal that&nbsp;
Halloran failed to convict, so Nelson made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.120
him a part of his imitation version of&nbsp;
John Kramer’s Barn Game. Each player of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.160
imitation game in 2016 bears a resemblance&nbsp;
to someone from the original game in 2003.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.960
We never see Malcolm Neale’s face, but we do see&nbsp;
the faces of Carly, from 2003 and her counterpart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.840
in 2016 and they are clearly not the same person.&nbsp;
I’m not allowed to show a dead body, but I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.000
show you credits, where Carly and Carly-look-alike&nbsp;
are played by different actresses.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.080
For some reason, Mitch’s look-alike is&nbsp;
not in the credits, but if you go on IMDb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.960
you can compare the two actors. They&nbsp;
don’t actually look that much alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.400
but since we only see Carly and Mitch’s&nbsp;
present day counterparts covered in blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.320
it’s a little harder to tell, but you definitely&nbsp;
will notice if you’re looking for it.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.080
It gets a bit convoluted when we find&nbsp;
out that Malcolm Neale’s counterpart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.480
from the past is actually Nelson himself, who&nbsp;
was put in the original barn game for messing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.240
up John Kramer’s X-rays, but was bailed out&nbsp;
because he didn’t wake up in time, resulting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.480
in some deep lacerations on his back, which we&nbsp;
see the scars of in the present day storyline.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:12.480
And that’s kind of confusing, because he&nbsp;
also put himself in the re-created game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.840
but his counterpart is Malcolm Neale… not himself.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.920
LOGAN: “Jigsaw put five people in his game. I put&nbsp;
in three. You and I were the last two.”

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.080
So Carly’s counterpart is Carly-look-alike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.920
Mitch’s counterpart is Mitch-look-alike,&nbsp;
Logan Nelson’s counterpart is Malcolm Neale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:32.080
Ryan’s counterpart is Logan Nelson, and&nbsp;
Anna’s counterpart is I guess Halloran.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.360
By itself, that might not be enough to&nbsp;
figure it out, but the existence of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.440
secret game that precedes the Saw saga&nbsp;
is dangled in front of us by Eleanor.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.920
“Supposedly John Kramer designed this trap for&nbsp;
a game that took place before all the others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.880
They found the plans in his&nbsp;
lab. I built it myself.”

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.400
Of course we see that same trap in the barn&nbsp;
game. Then when Eleanor and Logan go into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.440
the barn in the present, you can spot&nbsp;
one of the bucket masks near the door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.840
and it looks like it’s been&nbsp;
rusting there for years.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.840
So all of that adds up to make Jigsaw’s&nbsp;
twist ending kind of an homage to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:07.280
“this game already happened” twist seen&nbsp;
in Saw 2, although I do think it’s more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:10.800
satisfying in Saw 2 because we find out&nbsp;
the same way the characters find out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.280
whereas Jigsaw is told out of order just to&nbsp;
trick the audience. But I’ve come to appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.040
it as just being there to honor the history&nbsp;
of the franchise. There would be many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:23.794
secrets that pay homage to other aspects&nbsp;
of the Saw movies hidden inside of Jigsaw.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.000
A couple of the supporting characters in&nbsp;
Jigsaw seem to be spiritual successors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.840
to characters from the original series.&nbsp;
Detective Keith Hunt originally appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.160
to be the new version of SWAT Officer Daniel Rigg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.400
but we later find out he’s with Internal&nbsp;
Affairs just like Matt Gibson from Saw 7.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.200
There’s also an unnamed Doctor in&nbsp;
Jigsaw who looks like she could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.360
be the sister of Lynn Denlon from Saw&nbsp;
3. Here’s a side by side comparison.

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.160
Not sure if this is intentional, I&nbsp;
don’t know how many people do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.720
but the doctor is also wearing her watch on&nbsp;
the inside of her wrist, like John Kramer does.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.320
The game itself also shares a ton of&nbsp;
similarities to other traps and games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.680
that we’ve seen in the Saw series, and I think&nbsp;
that because the Barn Game was essentially a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.880
test for Jigsaw before taking his games public,&nbsp;
he would reuse many concepts in his later work.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.240
Let’s look at the first room. There&nbsp;
are many similarities to Saw 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.600
the most obvious being that we have five&nbsp;
players with chains around their necks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.920
are pulling them towards these dangerous blades.&nbsp;
Sixty seconds are given in both scenarios.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.920
JIGSAW: “Today, five will become one, with the common&nbsp;
goal of survival… However if one of you moves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.560
to retrieve the key, the sixty second&nbsp;
timer will begin, for you all.”

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.080
Maybe this is a foreshadowing of the fact&nbsp;
that the Barn Game requires the players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:46.160
to work together, which is also what the&nbsp;
Fatal Five was tasked with figuring out.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.680
“It was meant for five people.”

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.560
The final room of their trial asks for a blood&nbsp;
sacrifice, just like the blade room in Jigsaw.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.920
“The device before you is one of&nbsp;
sacrifice. A sacrifice of blood.”&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:03.920
“First, an offering of blood no matter&nbsp;
how little will give you a green light.”

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.320
The blood sacrifice is one of many examples&nbsp;
of Jigsaw intentionally making his teachings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.440
have these religious undertones. I’ve&nbsp;
pointed out examples throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:14.960
Saw Things You Missed episodes, but in&nbsp;
Jigsaw it’s on display more than ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.680
Listen to the way John Kramer&nbsp;
addresses his followers.

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:23.440
“Salvation can be yours if you cleanse&nbsp;
yourselves of the habitual lies which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.920
have brought you here... Confess.&nbsp;
The truth will set you free.”

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:33.040
He also speaks not of rehabilitating his victims&nbsp;
like he does during the Saw saga, but he mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.240
the need for them to release their demons.
“If you can release yourself from your demons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:42.240
you can begin to shoot the chains&nbsp;
that those demons bring with them.”

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:46.960
Previous Saw movies tied into religion because&nbsp;
John’s traps represented the idea of suffering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.280
as a form of repentance for one’s sins,&nbsp;
while Jigsaw focuses more on confession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.800
which is also what Logan’s final&nbsp;
ultimate plan is for Halloran.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.080
“Innocent people died because of me, I did it!”

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.520
The most important part of his revenge&nbsp;
is that he recorded this confession,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:05.280
and perhaps that is part of the reason that&nbsp;
this movie’s iteration of Billy the Puppet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.680
has the unique glowing red eyes -- a red&nbsp;
light is the universal sign for recording.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.400
I’ve also theorized that it could&nbsp;
be a foreshadowing of what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.800
to Carly after she’s injected with the acid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.960
it causes blood to seep from her eyes,&nbsp;
giving her the appearance of having red eyes.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:22.640
This happens after Jigsaw challenges her to&nbsp;
choose the needle that represents how much she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.880
values a human life, with each one labeled to&nbsp;
a specific dollar amount. The needle with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:31.120
correct answer contains an antidote. This is&nbsp;
a reference back to the Nerve Gas House Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.720
where players must compete to win antidotes&nbsp;
for a poison coursing through each of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:39.040
systems. There’s also a reference to the original&nbsp;
Saw; after hearing Jigsaw explain the rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.720
the test-subjects check themselves to try to&nbsp;
see if they’ve been injected with anything.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.280
“OK. Check yourself for marks. If he&nbsp;
injected us there could be marks.”

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.040
This is the same reaction Adam has&nbsp;
shortly after waking up in the bathroom.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.370
ADAM: “Can you see any scars?”

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:53.425
LAWRENCE: “What?”

00:09:53.425 --> 00:09:55.080
ADAM: “This is&nbsp;
what they do man. They kidnap you and drug

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.527
you. Before you know it you’re lying in&nbsp;
a bathtub and your kidneys are on eBay.”

00:09:59.527 --> 00:10:02.800
As I mentioned, Carly’s look-alike shares a&nbsp;
similar fate in the present; she is found to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.880
have been injected with hydrofluoric acid, the&nbsp;
same substance that Jigsaw would go onto use in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:10.960
Saw 6 when Hydrofluoric Acid is injected&nbsp;
into William Easton during The Zoo Game.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.839
TARA: “HF. What does that stand for?"

00:10:13.839 --> 00:10:15.279
BRENT:  "Hydrofluoric aric.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:19.120
This stuff will eat through&nbsp;
human flesh within seconds.”

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:23.680
The next trap of Kramer’s Barn Game was also&nbsp;
designed to drown us in Saw easter eggs. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:28.160
starts when Mitch and Anna go into a grain silo.&nbsp;
They are buried alive in corn kernels while Ryan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.400
is stuck on the outside with the ability to&nbsp;
save them at his own personal expense. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.680
is the exact setup that Jigsaw would one day use&nbsp;
for The Pig Vat Trap from Saw 3. In The Pig Vat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.120
Jeff Denlon must decide whether or not to&nbsp;
save the judge that sentenced his son’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.520
killer from being drowned in pig guts at the&nbsp;
cost of burning the child’s old possessions.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.440
There’s another reference to The Pig Vat Trap in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:52.211
Jigsaw. Remember that music I was&nbsp;
jamming out to in my Saw 3 episode?

00:10:52.589 --> 00:10:59.330
🎵 Music from Saw 3 🎵

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.360
For whatever reason, it’s not in the&nbsp;
Original Motion Picture Soundtrack for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.870
Saw 3. It may be because the riff is very&nbsp;
similar to Eyes of the Insane by Slayer…

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:15.354
🎵 "The eyes, of the insane! On a demented campaign!" 🎵

00:11:15.354 --> 00:11:18.800
So maybe they got a little nervous&nbsp;
about copyright, and decided not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.205
put that part in the score, but the good news&nbsp;
is there’s a more finished version in Jigsaw.

00:11:23.205 --> 00:11:30.160
🎵 Cycle Trap - Charlie Clouser 🎵

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.000
It plays during the Cycle Trap, where it's&nbsp;
mostly drowned out by sound effects and dialogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.920
but luckily it’s also in the end credits and&nbsp;
it did make it into the soundtrack this time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.240
I personally love when music has engine sounds&nbsp;
as part of the mix for some reason. NASCAR Type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.880
Theatrical Hybrid Event is probably my favorite&nbsp;
Dethklok song and Horsepower is the song that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.680
got me into Muzzy. Personally, I’m still holding&nbsp;
out for composer Charlie Clouser to play Knotfest.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:55.680
But the Grain Silo Trap has more than just&nbsp;
one easter egg to offer, because after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.600
thing fills up with kernels, sharp objects&nbsp;
begin to rain down on the characters. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.760
abandoned barn is owned by Jill Tuck’s family.&nbsp;
That’s Jigsaw’s ex-wife if you don’t remember.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.520
I’ve always wondered where John gets all the&nbsp;
weapons used to create his traps, and obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:11.520
they come from many sources, it seems like many&nbsp;
of them could have been tools at the pig farm.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:14.080
Among the items that fall are circular saw blades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.000
which are obviously a part of many traps,&nbsp;
most notably, the ending of Jeff’s Trial...

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.840
...knives, like those seen in&nbsp;
Saw 4’s Knife Chair trap...

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.840
...stakes, which play a vital&nbsp;
part in the Spike Trap from Saw 6…

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:32.640
...meat hooks, which are used&nbsp;
to hoist Bobby Dagen in Saw 7…

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:38.080
...and these mugs from the CZsWorld merch&nbsp;
sto-- wait! What is that doing th--

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.200
NEXT. TRAP. Actually before&nbsp;
we even get to the next room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:44.800
the dividers between these two rooms are&nbsp;
the exact ones from Gideon Meat Packing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.080
Plant in Saw 3 and 4. Just thought&nbsp;
I’d point that out. But moving on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.160
the next room is the Human Blender, and this&nbsp;
iteration of it is powered by a motorcycle.&nbsp;

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:56.000
This is Mitch’s test, because he sold John’s&nbsp;
nephew a motorcycle one time and the brakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.600
didn’t work and the kid died. So his goal&nbsp;
in this trap is to reach for the brake that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:03.760
will slow down this motorcycle. But that would&nbsp;
not be the last trap that John Kramer creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.760
where the subject has to reach into danger to&nbsp;
access the brake for an automobile. This pays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:12.240
tribute to the horsepower trap, the setup that&nbsp;
proves fatal for Evan and his friends in Saw 7.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.520
After Mitch stars in the world’s&nbsp;
scariest episode of Will It Blend?,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:20.960
only Anna and uhh… part of Ryan remain. They&nbsp;
are subdued by John Kramer in a pig mask, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:25.280
they next wake up in the slaughter room. I think&nbsp;
this reference will be fairly obvious to Saw fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:29.920
but they end up facing each other, each with an&nbsp;
ankle in a shackle on opposite sides of the room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.800
This scenario obviously calls back to Dr. Gordon&nbsp;
and Adam’s situation in The Bathroom Game.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.560
“I just woke up here, just like you.”

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:40.800
But the traps seen in John Kramer’s original&nbsp;
Barn Game weren’t the only nostalgic references&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:46.480
to traps and setpieces of the Saw saga that&nbsp;
ran from 2004-2010. From Eleanor’s Studio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:47.760
to Nelson’s apartment there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.099
are still plenty more Things&nbsp;
You Missed for us to explore.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:03.600
We can’t let Jigsaw have&nbsp;
all the fun in this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.960
because this movie represents the&nbsp;
passing of the torch to a new disciple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.280
Logan Nelson. Early on, we see part of the reason&nbsp;
that Nelson wants to test Detective Halloran.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:13.357
HUNT: “Why do you think he asked for you?”

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.164
HALLORAN: “We’re good friends. Put him away twice.”

00:14:16.596 --> 00:14:20.650
Halloran was a corrupt cop, and at the end of&nbsp;
the film, Nelson gets him to admit all of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:24.480
wrongdoing by making him think that Jigsaw&nbsp;
is back and threatening him with the laser&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.400
collar. We actually see this payoff setup&nbsp;
by Eleanor as they examine the first victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:31.840
she uses a singular laser cutter to&nbsp;
remove the bucket fastened to his head.&nbsp;

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.440
This does kind of raise some questions for&nbsp;
me, like if this things cuts through steel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:38.960
why doesn’t it cut all the way through the&nbsp;
building? But we’re not here to focus on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.240
we’re here to talk about the flash drive&nbsp;
that Logan pulls out of his victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.400
because it’s a reference to an earlier Saw movie.

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:46.400
You may remember when we talked about Saw 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:50.400
where the recently passed away Jigsaw has&nbsp;
one final message for Detective Hoffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:54.080
a tape that he covers in wax and swallows&nbsp;
for the pathologists to discover.

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.115
“Where is it?”

00:14:55.115 --> 00:14:56.264
“It was in&nbsp;
his stomach.”

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.840
When Logan discovers the flash drive, he&nbsp;
has to cut it out of the plastic wrapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:03.520
that it’s encased in before putting&nbsp;
in a computer to listen to the audio.

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:07.440
“The games have begun again.&nbsp;
And they will not stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.440
until the sins against the&nbsp;
innocent are atoned for.”

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.600
When they get the results&nbsp;
of the voice analysis back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:17.040
Logan echoes one of Jigsaw’s&nbsp;
mottos: we speak for the dead.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.920
“Just because someone is dead doesn’t&nbsp;
mean they can’t have a voice.”&nbsp;

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.240
“Give us enough time, we speak for the dead.”

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:25.680
It reminds me of the time Hoffman, who was&nbsp;
secretly Jigsaw’s successor at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:29.120
used John’s motto as part of his&nbsp;
speech at a police press conference.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.640
“Throughout this ordeal, if&nbsp;
we’ve learned anything it’s that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.720
human life is sacred. And we’ve learned&nbsp;
that every day, life should be cherished.”

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:42.960
And speaking of Hoffman, I’ve got a little&nbsp;
something for all you Hoffman is alive people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:46.640
There’s this scene where we see Hunt walking&nbsp;
through the police station, and in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:51.840
there’s a board of wanted posters. If Hoffman was&nbsp;
alive, don’t you think Hoffman would be on here?

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:56.560
The only possibility really is that he’s alive,&nbsp;
but he’s in jail. Last time we saw him he was left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.880
in The Bathroom to die. Now we know The Bathroom&nbsp;
was eventually discovered, because in Eleanor’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.960
studio there’s a replica of the hacksaws, which&nbsp;
were only ever used at The Bathroom. She also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:08.800
has a replica of The Magnum Eyehole Trap, which&nbsp;
is in the room with the stairs that lead down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.960
to the tunnel network where The Bathroom is.&nbsp;
Though it is possible that her recreation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.200
The Magnum Eyehole was based entirely off of&nbsp;
blueprints, but as far as the hacksaw goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.440
I can’t imagine there would be blueprints for&nbsp;
that. Plus, even if there were for some reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:25.680
she wouldn’t know they were significant unless&nbsp;
The Bathroom was discovered. My point is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:29.600
they probably did discover it, but Hoffman&nbsp;
was probably dead by the time they got there.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.600
Other than the Magnum Eyehole, there are&nbsp;
replicas of many other iconic Saw traps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:38.400
seen in Eleanor’s studio. There’s the Angel Trap,&nbsp;
which Detective Kerry was caught in during Saw 3…

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:43.600
The Water Cube that Special&nbsp;
Agent Strahm escaped in Saw 5…

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:47.440
The Reverse Bear Trap. This is the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:50.560
used against Amanda Young in&nbsp;
Saw, and Jill Tuck in Saw 7.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.680
This might be the Razor Box from Saw 2...

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:04.000
It’s hard to tell for sure, but I feel like&nbsp;
we will see this location again in a future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:08.080
film. The closet is a hidden door, which is&nbsp;
one of the recurring staples of the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:12.320
and in the closet there’s a copy of the knife on&nbsp;
a chain from the Pound of Flesh Trap in Saw 6.

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:17.440
The inside of the closet is a shrine to Jigsaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:21.280
and contains the actual newspaper headlines&nbsp;
seen throughout the seven past movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:24.880
She also has an actual evidence marker just&nbsp;
like those used at the Jigsaw crime scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.880 --> 00:17:28.960
and what appears to be the black hair from&nbsp;
either Billy the Puppet or the pig masks.

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:32.880
At one point we actually get a better look&nbsp;
one of the newspapers that appeared in Saw 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:37.200
but in that movie it was too blurry to actually&nbsp;
make out any of the text. It gives us a few more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.880
Saw 4 easter eggs, but I think it makes&nbsp;
sense that I include them in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:45.280
since they’re only readable in Jigsaw. They are,&nbsp;
as usual, the writers of these articles being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:49.600
named after the film crew of the fourth movie.&nbsp;
Ryan Hupponen was the art production coordinator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:54.160
Ashleigh Millar worked craft services in a rare&nbsp;
on screen appearance for the snack department and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:59.840
Pauline Quong was a payroll accountant. And no,&nbsp;
that is not a racial stereotype joke. Look it up.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:03.680
If you watched all the previous videos&nbsp;
in the Saw Things You Missed series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:08.000
you now know about a ton of those crew member&nbsp;
references across the series. Jigsaw does things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.520
a bit differently though. There’s only one&nbsp;
such reference that I noticed, and it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.040
just be a total coincidence and I’m looking too&nbsp;
much into it, but in the cemetery scene there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:19.520
a headstone labeled Armstrong. The biggest&nbsp;
difference between Jigsaw and the Saw movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:24.240
is the cinematography, and David Armstrong was the&nbsp;
cinematographer of Saws 1 through 6, and his style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.200
was emulated in Saw 7. So to me, this tombstone&nbsp;
represents the death of the Armstrong look.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.760
Or maybe they just filmed in an actual cemetery&nbsp;
and this happened to be there. If anyone lives in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:39.440
Toronto, let me know. I mean I know people live&nbsp;
in Toronto obviously.... You know what I meant.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:42.320
As far as references to other&nbsp;
stuff outside of the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:46.160
there’s not much. Unless you want to call this&nbsp;
a reference to Wolverine or Freddy Krueger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:50.320
I’m not sure if I want to. But there does&nbsp;
seem to be one reference to an outside work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:56.000
but I can’t take credit for finding&nbsp;
it. So I’m not going to. Roll the clip.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:59.520
“Apparently, critics have noted a&nbsp;
structural similarity between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:03.440
Saw films and French philosopher&nbsp;
Jean-Paul Sarcre’s famous play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:08.880
No Exit. So I watched all seven movies, read&nbsp;
No Exit, and immediately called bullsh**.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.880 --> 00:19:15.520
The connection was too tenuous. Then, Jigsaw&nbsp;
came out and when I saw this, I was like… huh.”

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.160
I’ll leave the link in the description&nbsp;
if you want to see his whole video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.760
but the basic gist of it is that this&nbsp;
play, No Exit is all about the torture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:26.080
of confessing and being judged for one’s sins&nbsp;
by others. That’s the reason that Jigsaw in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.760
this movie tortures his victims by asking&nbsp;
them to confess in front of the group as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:33.520
opposed to the physical torture seen in the&nbsp;
previous entries. Maybe that’s the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:36.960
that’s the reason Jigsaw’s credits bring back&nbsp;
the music from the Pig Vat Trap, because that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:40.960
like the one other trap in the series that&nbsp;
focuses much more on psychological torture.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.840
So Jigsaw redefines what it&nbsp;
means to experience torture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:48.539
and it would also redefine one of the long&nbsp;
running symbols of the franchise: The Pigs.

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:03.520
In a previous video, I mentioned that one of the&nbsp;
main characters in the movie, Detective Halloran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:08.960
doesn’t have a name other than… Halloran.&nbsp;
Well I’m proud to announce that I, CZsWorld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:15.040
have discovered Halloran’s first name. It’s Brad.&nbsp;
He’s a Brad. They say that finding this name is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:19.920
as rare as finding Obama’s last name, so make&nbsp;
sure you like this video, just for that alone.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:22.640
When Eleanor is putting together&nbsp;
the pathology report on the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:27.280
victim she discovers traces of pig fecal&nbsp;
matter found to contain Aujesky’s disease.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:29.920
ELEANOR: “When swine get it, farmers have&nbsp;
to wipe out the entire sounder.”

00:20:29.920 --> 00:20:32.160
In all of the other Saw movies, pigs represent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.840
rebirth of John’s followers. They are&nbsp;
metaphorically leaving their crimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:40.560
or at least what Jigsaw perceives as crimes, in&nbsp;
a previous life. It seems like in Jigsaw though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.760
the meaning of the pig symbol has been expanded&nbsp;
to where pigs are now also a sign of compassion.

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:50.720
“Did you know that pigs are highly compassionate&nbsp;
animals? They show distress if they see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:54.480
any other animal, including humans, suffering.”

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:58.000
John’s goal has always been to make&nbsp;
his subjects endure suffering open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.280
their eyes and make them appreciate&nbsp;
their lives again. If they survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:05.520
they become his followers, and wear the pig&nbsp;
masks to help spread John’s message to others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:08.960
That’s why it’s significant that the&nbsp;
barn game takes place in a pig farm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:13.040
Each of them represents one of his pigs,&nbsp;
they can either win and become his followers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:17.760
or lose and be slaughtered. The final room, which&nbsp;
was to be their final test, is the slaughter room.

00:21:17.760 --> 00:21:20.480
But throughout the game he doesn’t&nbsp;
actually have followers yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.840
so he’s seen wearing the pig mask&nbsp;
himself during the past storyline.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:28.240
In the present storyline, it appears that&nbsp;
John Kramer, or one of his disciples, is back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:33.120
again holding new games, and at the end of the&nbsp;
movie Logan and Eleanor go to hunt him down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.640
During these scenes, Eleanor is wearing&nbsp;
a leopard print jacket and leopards are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:41.920
a natural predator of many wetland hogs and&nbsp;
boar. In other words… leopards hunt pigs.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.760
And although that was Eleanor’s intention,&nbsp;
she ends up having to run from the pig farm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:50.560
where she’s picked up by a driver. We never see&nbsp;
who it is, but she looks kind of distressed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:56.720
confused when she sees the person. Could it be&nbsp;
Dr. Gordon? Daniel? Corbett? Only time will tell.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:01.360
“Literally. Cause I’m building&nbsp;
a time machine to find out.”

00:22:01.360 --> 00:22:03.600
Oh my God that’s a terrible idea!

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:06.880
So we eventually come to learn that&nbsp;
Logan Nelson was actually Jigsaw’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:10.560
apprentice all along, and we was putting&nbsp;
himself in the game at the end there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:14.160
just as John did, just as Amanda&nbsp;
did and just as Hoffman did.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:17.280
But how did he make this whole thing&nbsp;
work? Let’s look back at it. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:21.040
was a lot of background work required by&nbsp;
Nelson, such as sneaking back into St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:24.960
Peter’s Hospital to kidnap Edgar Munsen,&nbsp;
digging up John Kramer’s grave and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:29.280
depositing the bodies of his recreated game&nbsp;
throughout the city for Brad Halloran to find.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.840
I guess we are to believe that&nbsp;
he just stays up and does all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:35.120
this stuff overnight based on what&nbsp;
he tells his daughter’s babysitter.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.360
“I’m sorry I have to stay so late again Judy.”

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:40.560
There’s also a clue about him being&nbsp;
connected to Jigsaw in his study,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.600
where you can spot one of those little&nbsp;
Buddhist figures in his bedroom, just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.520
the ones first seen at John Kramer’s deathbed&nbsp;
among other appearances throughout the series.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:50.960
When Brad looks into Logan Nelson’s&nbsp;
bio on the Veterans Affairs database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:54.080
there’s a lot of background information&nbsp;
about his time during and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:58.480
serving in the Army. There’s a lot to cover here&nbsp;
that helps explain why he never helped Jigsaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:02.800
during Saw saga. If you’re interested, I made&nbsp;
a whole video on The History of Logan Nelson.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.000
When Eleanor shows him the studio, they’re&nbsp;
being tailed by Hunt, who I can already tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:10.320
is a better evidence photographer than Adam, cause&nbsp;
he doesn’t blatantly use the flash in a dark room.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:13.360
But Logan was still able to later&nbsp;
convince Hunt that Brad Halloran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:16.880
was setting him up. But by the time Hunt&nbsp;
gives his people the order to arrest Brad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:21.360
he’s already tailing Logan and Eleanor. They end&nbsp;
up essentially leading him into his own tomb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:26.160
I noticed that the situation is very similar&nbsp;
to what Hoffman does to Peter Strahm in Saw 5.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:30.480
Actually, it’s not very similar, it's exactly&nbsp;
the same. He makes J Jonah Erickson suspicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:34.720
of Strahm, he knows Strahm is following him&nbsp;
and he leads him right into his final trap.

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:38.640
Brad finds himself in the Slaughter Room, where&nbsp;
he and Logan both appear to be imprisoned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:43.200
these collars, and Logan uses an old Saw trick&nbsp;
to get him to make his confession. The lasers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:47.680
on Logan’s collar are fake, and so is the blood&nbsp;
that spurts out when the lasers zero in on him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:51.760
We’ve seen Adam do it, we’ve seen Jigsaw do it,&nbsp;
we’ve seen Daniel Matthews do it, we’ve seen Eric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:56.560
Matthews do it and now we would see Logan Nelson&nbsp;
play dead to get the upper hand on his opponent.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:00.240
As I mentioned way back at the beginning,&nbsp;
after a confession we see the Brad flower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:04.640
bloom into eight pedals as we come to&nbsp;
the end of the eighth movie. I have got.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:09.064
To get. A slidey. Door like that.&nbsp;
So I can game over all my guests.

00:24:09.172 --> 00:24:10.440
“I speak for the dead.”

00:24:10.711 --> 00:24:11.537
Or that.

